    "id": "5",
    "slug": "how-to-choose-restaurant-management-software-pakistan",
    "title": "How to Choose Restaurant Management Software in Pakistan",
    "excerpt": "Compare restaurant software in Pakistan using a practical checklist for POS, branded ordering apps, inventory, riders, pricing, support and branches.",
    "category": "Buying Guides",
    "author": "Restrova Team",
    "authorExpertise": "Restaurant software evaluation guidance from the Restrova team. Confirm product scope, device compatibility and support arrangements in a workflow-based demonstration and written proposal.",
    "date": "2026-10-27",
    "updated": "2026-11-02",
    "readTime": "5 min read",
    "relatedSolution": "restaurant-pos",
    "content": "A restaurant owner choosing software in Pakistan may be comparing very different products: a counter billing tool, a [point-of-sale system](/services/restaurant-pos), an online ordering website, a branded mobile app, inventory software or an all-in-one operations platform. The correct choice depends on the orders you handle, the staff using the tools and which channels you want to develop.\n\nStart by writing down what happens to an order at your restaurant today. Where does it arrive? Who accepts it? Who checks whether the item is available? How is the kitchen informed? How does the order reach a customer? What does the manager need to know at the end of the shift? Those details will make demonstrations more useful than a list of marketing features.\n\n## 1. Decide which orders the system must handle\n\nList counter, walk-in, phone, website, mobile-app, dine-in, takeaway and delivery orders separately. Ask each vendor which sources can enter its own system and which integrations require separate work. Check whether order statuses and menu changes are visible to the right staff.\n\nA system can support POS-related order entry without supporting every possible cash drawer, printer, payment provider or fiscal requirement. Ask for a demonstration using your own equipment and local compliance needs before signing.\n\n## 2. Decide whether you need your own restaurant app and website\n\nA marketplace listing and your own ordering channel serve different purposes. A restaurant-owned website or [branded Android/iOS app](/services/restaurant-ordering-apps) gives existing customers a way to find that restaurant's menu directly. A marketplace may still serve as a customer discovery channel.\n\nCheck the customer experience on a real phone: item categories, modifiers, availability, delivery or pickup options, checkout and order status. If branded mobile apps are important, ask which app stores and accounts will publish them, how updates are managed and what ongoing support is included.\n\n## 3. Test inventory against an actual menu\n\n[Inventory](/services/inventory-management) is only useful if staff can maintain accurate records. Choose a few common ingredients or products, agree their units and track starting stock, purchases, wastage and remaining stock. Ask the vendor to show how inventory and menu availability are connected, and what happens if a count is wrong.\n\nDo not assume automatic ingredient deduction, purchase ordering, recipe costing or accounting integrations are present just because the word “inventory” appears on a website. See the specific workflow in the demonstration.\n\n## 4. Verify delivery and rider responsibilities\n\nIf [your own team makes deliveries](/services/delivery-management), ask how orders are assigned to riders, how statuses change, who sees location information and how rider settlements are reviewed. Test the experience on the actual phones and network conditions you expect to use. Confirm permission, privacy and connectivity dependencies.\n\nCheck who is responsible when a customer provides an incorrect address, an order is cancelled or a rider is unavailable. Technology can improve visibility, but it does not remove operational responsibilities.\n\n## 5. Compare prices using your own sales volume\n\nAvoid judging a solution on a headline monthly fee alone. Compare onboarding, subscriptions, transaction-based fees, payment processing, app publishing, additional branches, hardware, training, support and cancellation terms. Ask which fees apply to all restaurant sales and which only to orders processed through the software.\n\nFor example, Restrova publishes a one-time onboarding fee of PKR 10,000 and a monthly fee equal to the higher of PKR 5,000 or 1% of monthly sales processed through Restrova. At PKR 300,000 of sales processed through the platform, 1% is PKR 3,000, so the minimum monthly fee of PKR 5,000 applies. At PKR 800,000, 1% is PKR 8,000, so that is the monthly platform fee. Sales processed outside Restrova are excluded from the percentage calculation. Confirm any separate charges in writing.\n\n## 6. Ask how multi-branch operations are configured\n\nA single-location restaurant may need only one menu and one team. A multi-branch business should ask about branch-specific menus, prices, availability, delivery areas, permissions and reporting. Ask to see how an owner switches branches and how staff access is restricted.\n\n## 7. Evaluate launch and support, not just features\n\nSoftware is a continuing operational responsibility. Ask who imports the menu, tests the ordering flow, trains staff, manages application updates and responds when an order fails. Confirm supported operating systems and internet requirements. Request a realistic written launch scope and evidence of the promised support process.\n\n## Restaurant software demo checklist\n\nBring a sample menu, an order containing modifications, a cancelled order scenario, an example stock adjustment, a delivery area and a sample end-of-shift report. Ask the salesperson to demonstrate each workflow. Record what works now, what requires configuration and what is not currently supported.\n\nCompare options against the problems you are solving, not against unverified claims to be the “best”. Restrova offers branded restaurant ordering apps, a direct ordering website and connected order, stock, rider and reporting workflows. Review [Restrova's services](/services), [ordering app capabilities](/services/restaurant-ordering-apps), [pricing](/pricing) and [FAQ](/faq). To assess fit for your own restaurant, [request a free demo](/contact).\n\nFor a detailed counter-service evaluation, use the separate [POS demonstration checklist](/blog/how-to-choose-restaurant-pos-software-pakistan). Learn [about Restrova](/about) and review [published app examples](/apps) before choosing the workflows for your demonstration.\n\n## Turn the shortlist into a decision\n\nUse the [ordering-system comparison worksheet](/blog/compare-restaurant-ordering-systems) to record demonstrated requirements and unresolved dependencies. For the budget, compare [the complete cost of restaurant software](/blog/restaurant-software-pricing-pakistan-guide) across the same branches and sales assumptions. If you manage several locations, prepare a [branch rollout worksheet](/blog/manage-multiple-restaurant-branches) before asking vendors to quote.",
