    "id": "1",
    "slug": "why-direct-ordering-beats-third-party-marketplaces",
    "title": "Restaurant Website vs Third-Party Delivery Marketplace in Pakistan",
    "excerpt": "Compare a restaurant website with delivery marketplaces in Pakistan using complete channel costs, discovery, rider responsibilities and a fair worked example.",
    "category": "Business Strategy",
    "date": "2026-02-01",
    "readTime": "5 min read",
    "image": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1556742049-0cfed4f6a45d?w=800&h=600&fit=crop&q=80",
    "relatedSolution": "restaurant-ordering-websites",
    "content": "A restaurant website and Foodpanda can do different jobs. A marketplace can help people discover restaurants; a direct ordering website gives customers a recognizable route to a particular restaurant. Choose the mix by comparing demand, service capacity and the complete cost of fulfilling orders. Direct ordering does not automatically produce a higher profit.\n\n## Start with discovery and repeat ordering\n\nFoodpanda's [Pakistan partner page](https://partner.foodpanda.pk/en) describes a marketplace ordering service with rider delivery. Whether that arrangement suits your restaurant depends on your agreement and coverage. A new restaurant may value discovery; an established restaurant may also want a direct link for guests who already know it.\n\nA website still needs visitors. Include the time and money spent explaining the official ordering link, maintaining profiles and encouraging repeat orders. Count completed orders rather than visits alone. If a customer moves from a marketplace to your website, that is a channel change, not necessarily additional restaurant revenue.\n\n## Check the actual marketplace package\n\nFoodpanda states that it offers different commission packages, with the applicable rate shown during registration. There is no single verified rate used in this guide. Read your proposal and settlement records for commission, promotions, payment handling, delivery, cancellations and adjustments.\n\nAsk which costs are included in a quoted percentage and which are additional. A package that includes delivery cannot be fairly compared with a software fee that leaves the restaurant responsible for riders. Use the same treatment of discounts and refunds on both sides.\n\n## List the direct-channel responsibilities\n\nA [restaurant ordering website](/services/restaurant-ordering-websites) needs an accurate menu, a staff member watching orders and a clear pickup or delivery process. If you use your own riders, budget their operating cost and review [dispatch and settlement](/services/delivery-management). Confirm payment-provider fees and what happens when payment succeeds but the order needs correction.\n\nRestrova's published fee is PKR 10,000 onboarding once, then the higher of PKR 5,000 or 1% of monthly sales processed through Restrova. That is a software fee, not a complete delivery or payment-processing budget. Confirm taxes, branch scope and exclusions in the proposal.\n\n## Worked example: compare the same 400 orders\n\nThis is a hypothetical planning exercise, not a Foodpanda quote, market benchmark or forecast. Assume 400 completed orders at PKR 1,000 each: PKR 400,000 in monthly sales. Assume equal food costs and order values for the exercise. Ignore tax, rent and other common overhead here, and list them separately before calculating profit.\n\n### Illustrative direct website costs\n\n- Restrova monthly software fee: max(PKR 5,000, 1% of PKR 400,000) = PKR 5,000.\n- Assumed payment cost of 2%: PKR 8,000.\n- Assumed restaurant delivery cost of PKR 100 per order: PKR 40,000.\n- Assumed customer-acquisition spend: PKR 20,000.\n- Assumed packaging cost of PKR 50 per order: PKR 20,000.\n- Total listed recurring channel costs: PKR 93,000, or PKR 232.50 per order.\n\nThe first month would also include PKR 10,000 onboarding, making the listed amount PKR 103,000 before any other charges. Replace the delivery assumption with the full relevant rider cost, including idle time, rather than counting fuel alone.\n\n### Illustrative marketplace costs\n\nFor comparison only, assume an unnamed marketplace package costs 20% and includes payment processing and delivery in that rate. This assumption is not a statement about Foodpanda's terms. The package would cost PKR 80,000. Add PKR 20,000 packaging and an assumed PKR 10,000 restaurant-funded promotion: total PKR 110,000, or PKR 275 per order.\n\nUnder these assumptions the direct channel's recurring listed costs are PKR 17,000 lower. If its delivery cost rises to PKR 150 per order, direct costs become PKR 113,000, which is PKR 3,000 higher. A smaller audience or higher acquisition cost can change the result again. Neither total is restaurant profit.\n\n## Manage customer relationships responsibly\n\nDirect ordering gives the restaurant a place to manage its own customer experience. It does not remove privacy duties or grant permission to reuse marketplace customer information. Check the applicable agreements and customer choices before sending promotions or directing people through another channel. Keep service messages and optional marketing distinct in your operating process.\n\nMake repeat ordering useful: maintain the menu, answer support questions and explain any loyalty offer clearly. Test the ordering journey before funding a promotion. An app download or coupon redemption alone does not establish a profitable repeat customer.\n\n## Decide what to test next\n\n- Compare one consistent month of completed orders and settlement records.\n- Write down what each provider includes and who handles delivery problems.\n- Replace every example assumption with your own quote or recorded cost.\n- Separate shifted orders from additional demand when reviewing growth.\n- Test a direct order through checkout, preparation, dispatch and closing.\n\nUse the [channel overview](/compare-marketplaces) for a quick comparison and the [pricing calculator](/pricing) for Restrova's software component. Bring your actual order mix and delivery plan to a [free Restrova demo](/contact).\n\n## Compare responsibilities alongside the fee\n\n| Question | Direct ordering website | Third-party marketplace |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| How do customers arrive? | Your restaurant's own links, reputation and promotion | The marketplace's discovery experience plus your listing |\n| Who maintains the menu? | Assign a restaurant owner and confirm the editing process | Confirm listing controls and responsibilities in the agreement |\n| Who handles delivery issues? | Your agreed dispatch, rider and customer-support process | Check which parts the actual package covers |\n| What cost evidence should I use? | Software proposal, payment statement and real operating costs | Your contract, settlement statement and promotion records |\n\nTreat these as questions to resolve, not promises that either channel includes every service. The [online ordering budget guide](/blog/restaurant-online-ordering-system-cost-pakistan) helps assemble the direct side of the comparison.\n\n### Can a restaurant use both channels?\n\nYes, a restaurant can evaluate both as part of its operating plan, subject to its agreements. Assign staff to watch each order source and keep availability current. Do not assume an order from a marketplace automatically enters Restrova; a specific import would require a supported integration.\n\n### How should we judge a trial?\n\nCompare completed orders, contribution after the costs you can attribute, failed fulfilment and staff workload over a defined period. Note promotions, menu changes and shifted orders that affect the comparison. Use the results to decide the channel mix rather than treating one cheaper hypothetical order as proof of a better business.",
    "author": "Restrova Team",
    "authorExpertise": "Product and operations guidance from the Restrova team.",
