    "slug": "restaurant-online-ordering-system-cost-pakistan",
    "title": "Restaurant Online Ordering System Cost in Pakistan",
    "seoTitle": "Restaurant Online Ordering System Cost in Pakistan",
    "excerpt": "Budget for restaurant online ordering in Pakistan: Restrova software fees, setup, payment charges, delivery, domain ownership and a practical cost worksheet.",
    "relatedSolution": "restaurant-ordering-websites",
    "content": "Restaurant online ordering costs depend on what the system includes and how the restaurant fulfils orders. Budget for setup, recurring software, payments and the people handling delivery or pickup. Restrova publishes PKR 10,000 onboarding once, then the higher of PKR 5,000 or 1% of monthly sales processed through Restrova. Those amounts are its software pricing, not a complete operating budget or a Pakistan-wide market average.\n\nUse the [published pricing and calculator](/pricing) for the current Restrova formula. This guide helps a restaurant owner turn that fee into a launch budget and compare proposals with the same assumptions.\n\n## Define the ordering service before asking for a price\n\nA menu website, a website with checkout and a connected ordering system are different scopes. Write down whether customers need to place orders, choose pickup or delivery, select item options and receive updates. Identify who at the restaurant receives and prepares each order.\n\nFor a takeaway starting with one branch, a practical brief might specify a mobile menu, required choices, an agreed payment method and a clear preparation handoff. A restaurant with several branches also needs to test order destination, menus and delivery boundaries. These are requirements to demonstrate, not automatic inclusions in every quote.\n\nReview the [Restrova ordering website workflow](/services/restaurant-ordering-websites), then record any app, counter, inventory or rider requirements in the same proposal. Do not assume adding another channel either creates a separate subscription or includes unlimited custom work.\n\n## Separate launch costs from recurring costs\n\n| Budget item | What to ask | Evidence to keep |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Setup and onboarding | Which menu, branding, branch and training tasks are included? | Written implementation scope |\n| Software | Fixed fee, sales percentage or minimum; when does billing start? | Fee formula and sales definition |\n| Domain and website operation | Who owns and renews the domain; what hosting and maintenance are included? | Account ownership and renewal responsibilities |\n| Payments | Which methods are supported; what provider charges apply? | Provider terms and reconciliation process |\n| Fulfilment | Who supplies riders or manages pickup? | Staffing and delivery budget |\n| Customer acquisition | How will customers find the official ordering link? | Restaurant's own promotion plan |\n| Other charges | What taxes, custom work or account fees are excluded? | Itemized proposal with unresolved amounts marked |\n\nAn unknown cost is not zero. Keep it open until the responsible party confirms it. Software that accepts orders does not itself supply food packaging, riders or an audience.\n\n## Calculate the software fee using the correct sales base\n\nRestrova's monthly formula is max(PKR 5,000, processed monthly sales × 1%). The minimum applies even if those sales are zero. At PKR 500,000, 1% equals PKR 5,000. The minimum and percentage are not added together.\n\n| Monthly sales processed through Restrova | Monthly software fee | Onboarding plus one month |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| PKR 200,000 | PKR 5,000 | PKR 15,000 |\n| PKR 500,000 | PKR 5,000 | PKR 15,000 |\n| PKR 800,000 | PKR 8,000 | PKR 18,000 |\n\nThese are illustrative software amounts before unconfirmed additional charges. If your setup also processes counter or app orders, do not assume website sales alone are the billing base. Agree branch coverage and the treatment of refunds, cancellations and adjustments in writing.\n\n## Work through one order-volume example\n\nAssume a hypothetical restaurant processes 200 completed orders at PKR 1,500 each through Restrova in a month, with no other Restrova-processed sales. Total processed sales are PKR 300,000. One percent is PKR 3,000, so the monthly software fee is PKR 5,000. Dividing that fee by 200 gives PKR 25 per completed order for software alone.\n\nThe first month also has PKR 10,000 onboarding, making the stated software total PKR 15,000. Neither figure includes food, packaging, payment charges, riders, promotion or unconfirmed taxes and extras. PKR 25 is not a delivery cost, profit margin or promised saving. Recalculate with your actual orders and complete sales base.\n\nFor a [website-versus-marketplace comparison](/blog/why-direct-ordering-beats-third-party-marketplaces), include the services each channel actually provides. A marketplace package with delivery and a software-only proposal need different supporting costs before they become comparable.\n\n## Compare proposals over quiet and busy months\n\nUse the same branch count, channels and service period. Calculate each month's variable fee separately, then add setup and other confirmed charges. Applying a percentage to an annual average can hide months when a minimum fee applies.\n\nAsk which responsibilities stay with your staff: menu entry, photography, accepting orders, handling failed payments, dispatch and support. Compare a total you can explain instead of choosing by the largest discount or the lowest headline fee. The [broader software pricing guide](/blog/restaurant-software-pricing-pakistan-guide) covers the whole restaurant setup; this worksheet focuses on the direct online channel.\n\n## Verify the paid scope with a demonstration\n\n- Open the menu on a phone and place an agreed test order with a required choice.\n- Confirm the order reaches the correct branch with accurate quantities and payment status.\n- Follow preparation into pickup or rider dispatch.\n- Test an unavailable item and ask how a correction reaches the final record.\n- Identify the report used to explain processed sales and the monthly fee.\n\nUse the [ordering system comparison checklist](/blog/compare-restaurant-ordering-systems) to record demonstrated features separately from promises. Keep the scope and acceptance results with the quote.\n\n## Common cost questions\n\n### Does a direct ordering website remove every commission or transaction fee?\n\nNo. A restaurant can have a software fee and separate payment or other charges. Restrova's published formula includes a sales-based component above its minimum threshold. Confirm the complete terms instead of interpreting direct ordering as fee-free.\n\n### Do we need to pay for a branded app immediately?\n\nChoose the channels your customers and team need. A browser-based website supports ordering from a link. If a branded app is part of the plan, use the [app development cost guide](/blog/restaurant-app-cost-pakistan) to agree store accounts, release work and maintenance within the combined scope.\n\n### What should I bring to a pricing discussion?\n\nBring your menu, branch count, expected processed sales, order sources and delivery arrangement. Mark estimates clearly. Follow the [online ordering launch checklist](/blog/start-online-ordering-small-restaurant-pakistan) and [book a free Restrova demo](/contact) to verify the workflow and written budget before launch.",
